    {
      "paper_id": "doi:10.1016/j.jue.2026.103868",
      "sequence": 10,
      "source_sequence": 10,
      "article_type": "research-article",
      "title_en": "JUE insight: City size, monopsony, and the employment effects of minimum wages",
      "title_cn": "JUE洞察：城市规模、垄断与最低工资对就业的影响",
      "authors": [
        "Priyaranjan Jha",
        "Jyotsana Kala",
        "David Neumark",
        "Antonio Rodriguez-Lopez"
      ],
      "abstract_en": "We assess how minimum wage effects on restaurant employment in the U.S. vary with market size and monopsony power. Using city-level data, we construct monopsony proxies based on labor flows and concentration. Minimum wages bind less in larger cities, consistent with the urban wage premium, and omitting this relationship overstates how labor market power reduces adverse employment effects of minimum wages. Nonetheless, accounting for size, lower labor market fluidity is linked to weaker employment effects, consistent with search models. By contrast, concentration-based proxies are less informative in this setting: employer-density measures move opposite to theory, while HHI exhibits insufficient variation.",
      "abstract_cn": "我们评估最低工资对美国餐馆就业的影响如何随市场规模和垄断势力的变化而变化。利用城市级数据，我们根据劳动力流动和集中度构建垄断代理。最低工资在大城市的约束力较小，与城市工资溢价一致，忽略这种关系夸大了劳动力市场力量如何减少最低工资的不利就业影响。尽管如此，考虑到规模，劳动力市场流动性较低与就业效应减弱有关，这与搜索模型一致。相比之下，在这种情况下，基于集中度的代理信息较少：雇主密度指标的变化与理论相反，而 HHI 的变化不足。",
      "abstract_snippet_en": "",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.jue.2026.103868",
