    {
      "paper_id": "doi:10.1016/j.jue.2026.103870",
      "sequence": 13,
      "source_sequence": 13,
      "article_type": "research-article",
      "title_en": "Fracking ghost towns: How neighborhood exposure drives population decline",
      "title_cn": "水力压裂鬼城：社区暴露如何导致人口下降",
      "authors": [
        "Arman Khachiyan"
      ],
      "abstract_en": "The fracking revolution has exposed more than 15 million American residents to drilling within 3 miles, a tripling from conventional extraction methods. While the economic benefits are distributed across nearby counties, the environmental and neighborhood costs are concentrated within miles of a well. Using granular data on exposure and outcomes, I show that nearby fracking activity shrinks neighborhood population by 5%. The spatial decay, temporal pattern, and mechanisms of this treatment effect operate to debunk the stylized argument that fracking creates local boom towns.",
      "abstract_cn": "水力压裂革命已使超过 15 百万 的美国居民在 3 英里内进行钻探，是传统开采方法的三倍。虽然经济效益分布在附近的县，但环境和社区成本却集中在井的几英里范围内。使用有关暴露和结果的详细数据，我发现附近的水力压裂活动使附近人口减少了 5%。这种处理效应的空间衰减、时间模式和机制揭穿了水力压裂创造当地繁荣城镇的程式化论点。",
